import { JsonReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> JsonReaderCopy = JsonReaderCopy {
  summary: "Use record to check a JSON object with a fixed field set. Its checkers share one success type, and successful results are name-and-value pairs in declaration-list order.",
  taskTitle: "Read two labels in a fixed order",
  purpose: "Allow only two string fields, first and second, in label settings. Even if JSON lists second before first, return the result in first-then-second order. Reject missing fields, extra fields, and values of the wrong type.",
  typescriptBefore: "The TypeScript version stores JSON.parse's result as unknown, checks for an object, then first rejects keys other than the two allowed names. It checks first and second as strings and builds an array of [name, value] pairs in the desired order. All supplied inputs are syntactically valid JSON.",
  benefit: "record takes a list of field names and checkers. That one list defines the allowed fields and result order, so callers do not repeat the unknown-key check and pair construction.",
  setup: "json is the alias for std/json. The annotation in let stringDecoder: json.Decoder<String> = decodeJson selects the string checker. (\"first\", stringDecoder) pairs a name with its checker. The bracketed list passes those pairs to json.record in first-then-second order.",
  after: [
    "labelsDecoder's type, json.Decoder<Array<(String, String)>>, describes a function reading Json into an array of name-and-string pairs. Its outer result is a failure in Left or a success in Right, with that array inside the success. The two Strings are the pair's name type and decoded-value type.",
    "fn report source: String -> String is an ordinary function. The colon gives the parameter type; -> gives the return type. Calls pass arguments with spaces, as in labelsDecoder value. match inspects parse's result and passes only Right value to labelsDecoder.",
    "For Right labels, encodeString displays the pair array. Although second comes first in the JSON, the result is [[\"first\",\"A\"],[\"second\",\"B\"]]. Left _ ignores the reason and returns Labels rejected; both remaining inputs are rejected.",
    "main's do runs println calls in order, and _ <- discards earlier print results. Unit means there is no useful return value; effect and with Console fails ConsoleError declare console access and possible printing failure. The record check itself is an ordinary function.",
  ],
  details: [
    "Every checker supplied to record must use the same success type A. Here A is String. For settings with mixed fields such as name: String and retries: Int, use a named struct with deriving JsonDecode. record returns an array of name-and-value pairs, not that kind of struct.",
    "Unknown fields are rejected before any declared-field checks. The last input has extra, an invalid first, and a missing second, but its first reason is extra. This short example ignores the detailed reason and displays every failure the same way.",
    "Without unknown fields, checks run in declaration-list order and stop at the first failure. All declared fields are required. To extract just one field while allowing unrelated fields, use field.",
  ],
}
